['```rust
// Define a struct to represent a packet with header and payload
struct Packet {
    header: Vec<u8>,
    payload: Vec<u8>,
}

// Function to extract a specified number of bytes from the payload of each packet
fn extract_payload_bytes(packets: &[Packet], num_bytes: usize) -> Vec<u8> {
    let mut extracted_bytes = Vec::new();

    for packet in packets {
        if packet.payload.len() >= num_bytes {
            // Extract the required number of bytes from the payload
            extracted_bytes.extend_from_slice(&packet.payload[..num_bytes]);
        } else {
            // If fewer bytes are available, extract all remaining bytes
            extracted_bytes.extend_from_slice(&packet.payload);
        }
    }

    extracted_bytes
}

// Example usage
fn main() {
    let packets = vec![
        Packet {
            header: vec![0x01, 0x23],
            payload: vec![0x45, 0x67, 0x89],
        },
        Packet {
            header: vec![0xAB, 0xCD],
            payload: vec![0xEF, 0xFE, 0xDC, 0xBA],
        },
    ];

    let extracted = extract_payload_bytes(&packets, 2);
    println!("{:?}", extracted); // Output: [0x45, 0x67, 0xEF, 0xFE]
}
```

This Rust function `extract_payload_bytes` takes a slice of `Packet` structs and an integer `num_bytes`, then returns a new vector containing up to `num_bytes` from the payload of each packet. The function ensures that it does not modify the original packets and handles cases where the requested byte count exceeds the available payload size gracefully.']

